                    briefing_text_r1:       `You hold 26 weeks of order-and-demand data at all four tiers. End-consumer demand varies only <strong>±6% a week</strong>, yet order variance grows to ±21% at wholesalers, ±34% at the DCs and <strong>±48% at the factory</strong> — and the 14% promotion lift spiked factory orders <strong>+62%</strong>. Measure the variance amplification ratio tier by tier, and rank the dominant cause among the four classics: <em>demand-signal processing</em>, order batching, price-promotion swings, and rationing/shortage gaming. Then state your opening hypothesis for the fix — and resist the obvious one.`,
